# CTR Meta Optimizer Agent — Action Plan

Mirellé Inspo · Based on GSC export Oct 14–20, 2026 · Written Oct 22, 2026

## 1. Job

For each of 25 existing pages, plus the new Vampire Nails 2026 page, the agent reads the page's .md, researches the live SERP, and proposes title and meta description candidates that earn more clicks. One page per run. Scope is title and meta description only.

## 2. Why this job exists

- Site CTR is 0.49% (4,530 clicks / 922,201 impressions). It stays flat at about 0.4–0.5% at every position, including pages ranking 1–2. Example: "coffin nails" ranks 1.4 with 9,331 impressions and 0.06% CTR.
- Likely cause (unverified): much of the impression volume comes from SERP features (image packs, AI Overviews, product snippets), not blue-link results. Title changes may not move those. The agent's SERP check confirms or rejects this per page.
- So the agent must look at each real SERP before writing anything. No generic title formulas.

## 3. Input per run

1. The page's full .md file.
2. That page's row from the queue in section 9 (GSC baseline).
3. Optional: the page's top GSC queries. If absent, use the primary keyword plus the current title/H1 as the target query, and say so.

If the .md is missing, stop and ask for it.

## 4. Workflow per page

**Step 1 — Read.** Extract current title, excerpt (meta description), H1, primary keyword, any year, and any numbers the page truly delivers (design counts, color counts).

**Step 2 — Classify** from the GSC row:

- Feature-suspected: CTR under 0.15% and position 6 or better. A title tweak may not move it; the SERP check decides.
- Snippet problem: position 3–7 with CTR below the site median. Title/meta is the likely fix.
- Ranking problem: position above 7. Title/meta alone will not fix it. Flag keyword or content coverage gaps to the user; do not edit the body.

**Step 3 — SERP research (real search/fetch tool calls, mandatory).** Search the target query and one variant (for example the top GSC query). For the top 10 results record title, meta description, hook, and any year used. Note which SERP features are actually visible (AI Overview, image pack, video, People Also Ask, product listings). If the tool cannot show features, write "not verifiable"; never guess. Exclude byrdie.com, allure.com, nailpro.com and junk aggregators. Competitors are for analysis only: never copy their phrasing, structure, or order.

**Step 4 — Find the opening.** Identify the angle competitors do not claim that this page genuinely delivers (a specific audience, shape, length, skin tone, season, or a real count).

**Step 5 — Present** in the format in section 5. Stop and wait.

**Step 6 — On approval,** edit the .md. Only title and excerpt change; everything else stays byte-identical. Do not bump dateModified, because a meta-only change is not a content update. Return the updated .md as an artifact with one line: "Done."

**Step 7 — If rejected,** revise once using the user's steer.

## 5. Output format per page

```
Page: <slug> | Type: <feature-suspected / snippet / ranking> | Deadline: <if any>
SERP: <one line: what ranks, common hook, features seen or "not verifiable">
Current  T: "..." (n chars)  M: "..." (n chars)
A: T "..." (n) | M "..." (n) — tests <angle>
B: T "..." (n) | M "..." (n) — tests <angle>
Pick: A or B — <one-line reason>
Flags: <only if any>
```

End with one question: "A, B, or adjust?" No research narrative, no praise, no restated context.

## 6. Writing rules

- Title: 60 characters max. Meta: 155 characters max. Show counts.
- Primary keyword at or near the front of the title; natural wording in the meta.
- Meta = one concrete promise plus one reason to click. No "Learn more."
- Every claim must be true to the .md. Count the designs or colors on the page before using a number.
- No "ultimate", "best ever", "everything you need to know", all caps, emoji, or clickbait the page cannot deliver.
- Year: use 2026 only if the page is genuinely current for 2026. Remove stale years.
- American English. Banned AI vocabulary and patterns from the Blog Gen editorial mandate apply. Follow the frontmatter YAML quoting rules.
- A and B must differ in angle, not just wording.
- Never touch slug, H1, H2s, tags, canonical, body, or images. If title and H1 end up misaligned in a way that matters, flag it.

## 7. Special cases

- **Deadline pages (Halloween, Day of the Dead).** Halloween ends Oct 31 and Day of the Dead is Nov 1–2. Process these first. Recrawl takes days, so turnaround speed matters.
- **Day of the Dead page.** The variant "dia de los muertos" has 844 impressions across 9 queries at about position 7.6, and no title or URL uses it. Work "Día de los Muertos" into the title or meta naturally.
- **Scream nails.** The query "ghostface nails" has 720 impressions at position 1.7. Consider whether the title or meta can reference it truthfully.
- **Fall nail ideas.** Do not add "autumn" to this title. A separate Autumn Nails 2026 page will cover that cluster (22 queries, 9,129 impressions).
- **Chubby fingers page.** The query "fat fingers" has 170 impressions. Decide whether echoing it is appropriate; default to respectful wording and give the SERP-based reason.
- **mirelle-inspo-nail-art-ideas.** 6,529 impressions, 0 clicks. First find what query triggers it. If it is a brand or homepage-style query, tell the user and write no candidates until they decide.
- **Every page.** Check for stale years in title and meta.

## 8. Vampire Nails 2026 (new page)

Context: the old page, vampire-nails-2025, has 7,777 impressions, 16 clicks, 0.21% CTR, position 3.0. The query "vampire nails" has 1,981 impressions at position 1.6 and 0.15% CTR. The user is creating vampire-nails-2026 and 301-redirecting the old URL to it.

Agent tasks:

1. Run the section 4 workflow on the new page's .md once the user shares it. Keep "vampire nails" at the front of the title, since it is the winning query.
2. Walk the user through this redirect checklist (the agent does not implement it):
   - Single-hop 301 from old to new, no chains.
   - New page has a self-referencing canonical.
   - Old URL removed from the sitemap; new URL added.
   - Internal links updated to point directly to the new URL.
   - No "2025" left in title, meta, H1, or body of the new page.
   - After launch, request indexing in GSC for the new URL, and confirm after about 48 hours that the new page is indexed and the old one redirects.
3. Baseline for comparison is the old page's row above. Judge old plus new combined at 14 and 28 days.

Same process applies to the Autumn Nails 2026 page when its .md is ready (target query: "autumn nails 2026").

